"Predicting Privileged Information Effectiveness for On-Policy Self-Distillation (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22512v1)\nciteturn41268view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22512v1\",\"lineno\":245}); Total lines: 1041\nL228: hurts its performance on ALFWorld and AppWorld, while on $\\tau^{2}$, adding either observations or thinking tokens causes even larger declines in Figure cite119†2(c) . Its high pass@4 of $88.9$ on $\\tau^{2}$ suggests that it already reasons effectively, leaving additional trajectory context less room to help and more potential to interfere with its existing strategy.\nL229: ##### Effective PI provides context beyond the correct solution.\nL230: Skills, which augment the solution with mistake analysis and reusable strategies, are often the strongest hint format. They lead for all three students on ALFWorld, both 4B students on AppWorld, and 4B-Instruct on $\\tau^{2}$.\nL231: Together with the gains from adding environment observations to trajectories, these results suggest that effective PI should do more than reveal or summarize a correct solution: it should provide context that helps the student understand its mistakes and how to act successfully in the environment.\nL232: ##### The PI source matters, especially when generating hints.\nL233: Figures cite120†2(d) –cite121†2(f) show that GLM-5.2 PI almost always outperforms Qwen3.7-Plus PI: for 4B-Instruct, GLM wins 20 of 21 comparisons, and its lead reaches 36.0 points on ALFWorld hints but stays within 3.2 points for trajectory formats. Source choice thus matters most when trajectories are rewritten into guidance: for trajectory PI, the source determines only the reference behavior the teacher sees, whereas for hints the generator also decides which information is kept and how it is expressed.\nL234: ##### Self-generated hints yield positive performance gains.\nL235: \nL236: For 4B-Instruct, both self-generated Summary and Skill hints improve performance on all three benchmarks (Figures cite122†2(g) –cite123†2(i) ). These results suggest that the student can act as its own hint generator given the reference solution and still benefit from OPSD.\nL237: ### 3.2 What Do PI Metrics Reveal?\nL238: Given the variation across PI configurations above and the substantial cost of training each one, we next ask whether post-training effectiveness can be assessed without running OPSD for every candidate. Figure cite113†3 plots each PI metric against post-training performance for every student under each PI configuration across ALFWorld, AppWorld, and $\\tau^{2}$-retail. All diagnostics are computed on training or development tasks disjoint from the post-training evaluation sets.\nL239: These comparisons show how utility, the distributional-shift statistic $D$, and the error-step contrast $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$ relate to the performance gains produced by different PI configurations.\nL240: Figure 3: Offline PI metrics versus post-training OPSD performance; columns correspond to ALFWorld, AppWorld, and $\\tau^{2}$-retail. Offline metrics are computed on training or development tasks for all three benchmarks. (a)–(c) PI utility. (d)–(f) Sampled-token reverse KL $D$. (g)–(i) Error-step KL contrast $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$. Lines show per-model linear fits; $\\rho$ denotes Spearman correlation.\nL241: \nL242: (a)\nL243: \nL244: (b)\nL245: \nL246: (c)\nL247: \nL248: (d)\nL249: \nL250: (e)\nL251: \nL252: (f)\nL253: \nL254: (g)\nL255: \nL256: (h)\nL257: \nL258: (i)\nL259: ##### PI utility can be inflated by information leakage.\nL260: The PI utility measures the improvement in the success rate of a model when conditioned on a given PI. We find that this metric can reward PI that reveals answer-specific information unavailable to the student, rather than PI that provides a transferable training signal. On AppWorld, the utility metric correlates strongly with post-training performance for all three students ($\\rho=0.81$–$0.96$).\nL261: The relationship is less consistent on ALFWorld, where the correlation is positive for 4B-Instruct ($\\rho=0.46$) but negative for 4B-Thinking and 30B-Instruct ($\\rho=-0.56$ and $-0.10$), and on $\\tau^{2}$, where correlations range from $-0.06$ to $0.71$. On ALFWorld, the PI-conditioned model can copy the reference trajectory’s final action without reproducing the preceding interaction sequence.\nL262: On $\\tau^{2}$, it can extract an answer from PI without executing the actions required to update the environment’s database (Appendix cite64†D ). In these cases, utility measures the model’s ability to exploit leaked solution information rather than how well the PI transfers through training.\nL263: Takeaway. PI utility is unreliable when information leakage changes evaluation behavior without providing transferable guidance, so higher utility does not imply larger OPSD gains.\nL264: ##### Larger distributional shifts usually accompany better performance.\nL265: We next examine $D$, the mean absolute sampled-token reverse KL between the student and PI-conditioned teacher. It correlates positively with post-training performance in eight of the nine student–benchmark combinations ($\\rho=0.65$–$0.95$). The exception is 4B-Thinking on $\\tau^{2}$, where the correlation is strongly negative ($\\rho=-0.84$). Its pass@4 of $88.9$ suggests that a large shift can overwrite useful behavior already present in the policy.\nL266: Our PI ablations point the same way: the student’s own failed rollouts induce a larger $D$ than its successful ones, even though they make far worse PI (Appendix cite68†E ). Thus, shift direction and location matter in addition to magnitude.\nL267: Takeaway. A larger $D$ generally indicates a stronger PI training signal, but does not reveal whether the induced shift improves the policy.\nL268: ##### Error-step KL contrast consistently tracks post-training performance.\nL269: Across all nine student–benchmark combinations, $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$ is positively correlated with post-training performance ($\\rho=0.10$–$0.94$). A larger contrast means that PI more strongly suppresses the student’s sampled tokens at judge-identified error steps than at comparable positions from the same task. Thus, PI formats that concentrate the distributional shift at critical mistakes tend to produce larger training gains.\nL270: This relationship is particularly strong for 4B-Instruct on AppWorld ($\\rho=0.94$) and 30B-Instruct on ALFWorld ($\\rho=0.92$). On $\\tau^{2}$, the contrast remains positively associated with performance for 4B-Thinking ($\\rho=0.70$), even though several trajectory formats have negative values. These small or negative contrasts and limited OPSD gains suggest poorly targeted supervision. This interpretation is consistent with cite124†Kaur et al.\nL271: [9] , who show that privileged distillation can penalize productive reconsideration tokens in thinking models: supervision may insufficiently suppress errors while disrupting useful behavior.\nL272: Takeaway. Effective PI concentrates token-level suppression on actual mistakes rather than comparable non-error steps, yielding a larger positive $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$.\nL273: ## 4 How Well Does OPSD Combine with GRPO?\nL274: \nL275: Figure 4: OPSD+GRPO performance on ALFWorld, AppWorld, and $\\tau^{2}$-retail. The top row shows performance across objective weights, and the bottom row shows training curves at $\\lambda=0.9$. Here, $\\lambda=0$ is vanilla OPSD and $\\lambda=1$ is GRPO; dashed lines show the matched GRPO baseline.\nL276: ### 4.1 Combined Training Objective\nL277: \nL278: Rather than optimizing over the many possible ways to combine token-level distillation and GRPO outcome rewards [cite125†21 ], we use a simple weighted objective to test whether PI identified as effective under vanilla OPSD remains effective when combined with GRPO. We use Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 for all experiments in this section. We combine the GRPO and OPSD signals at the advantage level:\nL279:  | $$A^{\\mathrm{OPSD+GRPO}}=\\lambda A^{\\mathrm{GRPO}}+(1-\\lambda)A^{\\mathrm{OPSD}},$$  |  | (7)\nL280: \nL281: where $A^{\\mathrm{GRPO}}$ is the outcome-based group-relative advantage, $A^{\\mathrm{OPSD}}$ is the token-level self-distillation advantage, and $\\lambda\\in[0,1]$ controls their relative weight.\nL282: \nL283: Figure cite126†4 compares four values of $\\lambda$ across three PI formats with matched GRPO-only controls on ALFWorld, AppWorld, and $\\tau^{2}$. The $\\lambda=0$ endpoint corresponds to vanilla OPSD.\nL284: ### 4.2 Effect of Objective Weighting\nL285: ##### PI quality persists: better PI is still better after adding GRPO.\nL286: \nL287: Trajectory PI performs best under vanilla OPSD and remains strongest across most values of $\\lambda$, followed by Skill and Checklist PI. This ordering is consistent through $\\lambda=0.99$ on all benchmarks. Thus, combining OPSD with outcome-based rewards does not eliminate differences in PI quality: stronger PI continues to provide a more effective auxiliary training signal.\nL288: Takeaway. The PI format that performs best under vanilla OPSD generally remains the strongest after adding GRPO, so outcome rewards complement rather than replace effective PI selection.\nL289: ##### GRPO strengthens OPSD.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the SPOT: Enhancing Speculative Decodingfor Diffusion Language Modelsvia Commit-Order Prediction (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543)\nciteturn41268view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543\",\"lineno\":265}); Total lines: 1284\nL250: The table is shared by drafter and verifier and indexed by absolute position, so the same position uses the same Gumbel row in both.\nL251: For target state $x$, the target draw is $v^{\\star}_{G}(x)=\\argmax_{v\\in\\mathcal{V}}\\left[\\frac{1}{T}\\log p_{\\theta,s^{\\star}(x)}(v\\mid x,C)+G_{a(s^{\\star}(x)),v}\\right]$, which defines the transition $\\Phi_{G}(x)=x\\oplus\\bigl(s^{\\star}(x),v^{\\star}_{G}(x)\\bigr).$ By the Gumbel–argmax identity, $v^{\\star}_{G}(x)\\sim\\pi_{T,s^{\\star}(x)}(\\cdot\\mid x,C)$. The drafter applies the same noise to its stale conditional:\nL252:  | $$\\widetilde{v}_{G}(s)=\\argmax_{v\\in\\mathcal{V}}\\left[\\frac{1}{T}\\log\\widehat{p}_{t,s}(v)+G_{a(s),v}\\right].$$  |  | (4)\nL253: \nL254: Thus, whenever the stale and fresh conditionals have the same Gumbel-perturbed argmax, drafter and target propose exactly the same token. Conditioned on $G$, $\\Phi_{G}$ is deterministic, so verification again reduces to state equality: $z=\\Phi_{G}(y).$\nL255: ###### Proposition 2 (Coupled-noise exactness).\nL256: \nL257: For any drafter $\\mathcal{D}$ and $T>0$, the Gumbel-driven draft-free trajectory satisfies $X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}(G)\\overset{d}{=}X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}$, and for every realization $g$ of $G$, $X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}(g)=X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}(g)$. Consequently, $X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}\\overset{d}{=}X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}$.\nL258: Propositions cite153†1 and cite154†2 therefore separate correctness from draft quality: the verifier always preserves the target trajectory or distribution, while the drafter determines only how many future target transitions are already present in the tree and can be accepted in one target forward. The complete formal setup and proofs are given in Appendix cite34†A . For a drafted position $s$, let $\\widehat{\\pi}_{T,s}$ and $\\pi_{T,s}$ denote the stale and fresh temperature-adjusted conditionals.\nL259: The shared-Gumbel coupling of cite141†Daliri et al. (2025) satisfies $\\Pr[\\widetilde{v}_{G}(s)=v^{\\star}_{G}(s)]\\geq\\frac{1-\\delta_{T}}{1+\\delta_{T}},$ where $\\delta_{T}=D_{\\mathrm{TV}}\\!\\left(\\widehat{\\pi}_{T,s},\\pi_{T,s}\\right).$ Hence coupled verification becomes more effective as the stale and fresh sampling distributions become closer.\nL260: #### Relation to rejection sampling.\nL261: When the drafter exposes an explicit sequential proposal distribution, exact verification can alternatively be performed by rejection sampling. Appendix cite51†B formalizes this alternative, proves its exactness and maximal per-link acceptance probability $1-D_{\\mathrm{TV}}(\\pi_{k},q_{k})$, and validates its implementation. Classical per-link RS assumes a sequential proposal interface, whereas coupled-noise match requires only state equality and therefore applies directly to our batched draft trees.\nL262: ## 5 Experiments\nL263: \nL264: We establish that SPOT takes fewer target forwards with higher throughput than every baseline at $T{=}0$ and $T{=}1$ (Section cite27†5.2 and Section cite28†5.3 ), then a checkpoint transfers across targets without additional training (Section cite29†5.4 ), and finally each component of the drafter earns its place (Section cite30†5.5 ).\nL265: ### 5.1 Experimental setup\nL266: Our headline target is SDAR-8B (cite119†Cheng et al., 2026 ), a dense block-causal diffusion LM at block width $L{=}32$ with its exact prefix KV cache. The same recipe gives drafters for SDAR-4B and SDAR-1.7B and for the mixture-of-experts LLaDA2.0-mini (cite147†Bie et al., 2025 ), and transfer is measured on two further targets, LLaDA-8B (cite117†Nie et al., 2025 ) and Dream-7B (cite120†Ye et al., 2025 ): six diffusion language models in all.\nL267: We evaluate GSM8K (cite155†Cobbe et al., 2021 ), MBPP (cite156†Austin et al., 2021b ), HumanEval (cite157†Chen et al., 2021 ) and MATH500 (cite158†Hendrycks et al., 2021 ) on $n{=}350$ samples (all $164$ for HumanEval) over three seeds, except where a caption says otherwise, at generation length $256$ and budgets $D\\in\\{8,12,20\\}$ and report NFE per sample, accuracy, and a throughput ratio against the draft-free run of the same task, model and temperature.\nL268: SPOT is one checkpoint and one calibration per target, and SPOT-$\\gamma$ runs it unpadded with that floor. Baselines are Spiffy’s calibrated draft graph (cite129†Agrawal et al., 2026 ), an SSD-style confidence-ordered free chain (cite128†Gao et al., 2025 ) and a BlockSpec-style confidence tree (cite124†Pan et al., 2025 ), all sharing our decoder and verifier so that the comparison is about drafting alone.\nL269: Other block widths, temperatures, and generation lengths, and the full protocol, are in Appendices cite67†D , cite79†E and cite80†F .\nL270: ### 5.2 Main results\nL271: Table 2: Main results, SDAR-8B b32 at $T{=}0$ and $T{=}1$: NFE per sample (throughput ratio over the shared draft-free control), best in bold, second best underlined. Shaded rows give each block’s accuracy range. SPOT-$\\gamma$: unpadded, $\\gamma{=}0.1$. At $T{=}1$ all methods use coupled-noise match except the free chain (RS). Over seeds the ratio’s standard deviation is at most $0.15$ and NFE’s at most $1.3$.\nL272: NFE$\\downarrow$ (tok/s$\\uparrow$)  | GSM8K  | MBPP  | HumanEval  | MATH500\nL273: method  | $D8$  | $D20$  | $D8$  | $D20$  | $D8$  | $D20$  | $D8$  | $D20$\nL274: $T{=}0$  | acc. 0.89–0.90  | acc. 0.41–0.43  | acc. 0.74–0.74  | acc. 0.11–0.11\nL275: no draft ($D0$)  | 270.8$\\downarrow$ (1.00$\\uparrow$)  | 269.2$\\downarrow$ (1.00$\\uparrow$)  | 271.4$\\downarrow$ (1.00$\\uparrow$)  | 271.8$\\downarrow$ (1.00$\\uparrow$)\nL276: SPOT (ours)  | 69.6 (2.50)  | 52.6 (1.56)  | 56.7 (2.61)  | 39.6 (1.72)  | 62.2 (2.74)  | 44.6 (1.85)  | 84.5 (2.03)  | 66.0 (1.20)\nL277: SPOT-$\\gamma$ (ours)  | 75.1 (2.76)  | 67.8 (2.78)  | 60.7 (2.98)  | 47.8 (2.99)  | 66.5 (3.09)  | 56.4 (3.13)  | 92.2 (2.27)  | 89.5 (2.27)\nL278: Spiffy graph  | 91.3 (1.96)  | 71.9 (1.14)  | 83.4 (1.86)  | 61.3 (1.17)  | 86.8 (2.11)  | 65.9 (1.28)  | 99.4 (1.80)  | 82.7 (0.99)\nL279: SSD free chain  | 92.2 (1.95)  | 84.9 (1.00)  | 84.7 (1.80)  | 76.1 (0.91)  | 87.0 (2.08)  | 80.2 (1.03)  | 100.5 (1.80)  | 93.6 (0.88)\nL280: BlockSpec tree  | 136.4 (1.33)  | 114.6 (0.71)  | 135.0 (1.10)  | 111.5 (0.58)  | 136.0 (1.34)  | 112.7 (0.73)  | 136.7 (1.29)  | 116.1 (0.68)\nL281: $T{=}1$  | acc. 0.87–0.90  | acc. 0.34–0.38  | acc. 0.66–0.72  | acc. 0.10–0.12\nL282: SPOT (ours)  | 72.7 (2.46)  | 54.8 (1.55)  | 60.1 (2.45)  | 42.6 (1.59)  | 65.1 (2.73)  | 47.4 (1.74)  | 87.6 (1.97)  | 68.9 (1.18)\nL283: SPOT-$\\gamma$ (ours)  | 78.4 (2.67)  | 71.0 (2.66)  | 64.9 (2.80)  | 52.1 (2.86)  | 69.5 (3.04)  | 59.3 (3.09)  | 95.4 (2.16)  | 92.7 (2.16)\nL284: Spiffy graph  | 94.3 (2.03)  | 75.5 (1.20)  | 86.3 (1.77)  | 64.8 (1.07)  | 89.4 (2.16)  | 68.9 (1.31)  | 103.5 (1.72)  | 87.7 (0.93)\nL285: free chain, RS  | 90.0 (1.54)  | 84.4 (0.85)  | 64.5 (1.86)  | 52.4 (1.12)  | 78.0 (1.84)  | 70.4 (1.05)  | 116.9 (1.14)  | 115.8 (0.60)\nL286: BlockSpec tree  | 139.3 (1.27)  | 118.2 (0.68)  | 138.3 (1.07)  | 115.2 (0.57)  | 138.4 (1.34)  | 115.5 (0.72)  | 140.7 (1.28)  | 121.0 (0.68)\nL287: In the upper block of Table cite144†2 , SPOT delivers $3.2$–$4.7\\times$ fewer target forwards than draft-free decoding at $D8$ and $4.1$–$6.8\\times$ at $D20$, at $2.0$–$2.7\\times$ single-device wall at $D8$; against Spiffy’s calibrated graph it has $15$–$35\\%$ fewer NFE and $1.1$–$1.5\\times$ its throughput in every task-and-budget setting, with larger margins against the chain and the tree.\nL288: The baselines’ throughput collapses with budget where their NFE does not: the tree is slower than draft-free at $D20$ on every task and the chain on three of four, since a wider draft costs $(1{+}D)L$ positions whether or not it is accepted, while they accept only $1.1$–$4.0$ tokens per round against SPOT’s $4.5$–$7.7$ (Appendix cite93†F.8 ).\nL289: SPOT-$\\gamma$ removes the budget dependence: its throughput ratio is flat in the budget ($2.3$–$3.2\\times$ at $D8$, $D12$ and $D20$ alike) and at or above the padded recipe in every SDAR-8B setting at both temperatures. The floor pays $4$–$24$ NFE per setting, so SPOT stays NFE-optimal while SPOT-$\\gamma$ still has fewer forwards than every baseline in $21$ of $24$ settings.\nL290: MRP (cite123†Xu et al., 2026 ), the closest learned drafter, reports $1.17$–$1.40\\times$ over one-token-per-step decoding on these same targets in a different implementation, against SPOT’s $2.0$–$3.6\\times$ at $D8$, a margin wider than that difference can account for, and its argmax-equality acceptance has no distribution-preserving extension to sampling (Table cite159†5 ).\nL291: ### 5.3 Sampling temperature: coupled-noise match against the baselines\nL292: The lower block of Table cite144†2 shows that nothing changes when the target samples at $T{=}1$: under the same verifier, SPOT keeps $15$–$34\\%$ fewer NFE than Spiffy and $1.15$–$1.48\\times$ its throughput. Free chain under rejection sampling is the strongest baseline on MBPP NFE, but stays $0.47$–$0.92$ below SPOT in throughput, since every accepted link pays an acceptance test, and collapses on MATH500 ($116$ NFE, $0.60\\times$ at $D20$).\nL293: Every $T{=}1$ result is distribution-preserving, with accuracies within $0.06$ of the control over seeds; tree $+$ coupled-noise match is the deployment configuration at any temperature (Appendix cite92†F.7 ). The ratios grow with generation length: at $512$ SPOT reaches $2.79\\times$ draft-free at $D8$ and SPOT-$\\gamma$ $3.23\\times$, against $2.45\\times$ and $2.86\\times$ at $256$ (Appendix cite87†F.5 ).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStructured Authority Injection: Persistent Human Control for LLM Agents via Typed MCP Overrides (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22373v1)\nciteturn41268view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22373v1\",\"lineno\":218}); Total lines: 298\nL206: ### IV-C Authority Persistence\nL207: \nL208: cite70†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: Robot time-state distribution across scenarios. cite71†Image: Refer to caption Figure 4: Robot time-state distribution across LLMs.\nL209: Figure cite72†3 breaks down robot time across four mutually exclusive states averaged over all nine LLMs per scenario-condition pair, including Working (executing a task on-site), Moving (traveling toward an assignment), Stuck (assigned but physically blocked for that tick by a collision or obstacle), and Pure Idle (no current assignment).\nL210: Pure Idle dominates in every scenario ($36$–$87\\%$), a structural consequence of the batched planning loop and post-completion dead ticks; high idle therefore coexists with perfect success rates and does not imply planning failure. The informative cross-condition signals are in Moving and Stuck.\nL211: SAI sharply increases Moving in S4 ($26\\%\\!\\to\\!44\\%$) as the late interrupt forces robots to re-navigate to reprioritised targets, and nearly eliminates Stuck in S3 ($9\\%\\!\\to\\!0\\%$) where structured zone constraints resolve baseline spatial conflicts. S7 carries the highest Stuck burden overall ($\\approx\\!27\\%$ baseline), while S5 shows the broadest activation increase ($13\\%\\!\\to\\!30\\%$), consistent with the completion gains in Table cite68†II .\nL212: Figure cite73†4 applies the same decomposition by LLM across all eight scenarios. Working time is near-uniform ($6$–$10\\%$) across all models, confirming that on-task execution is model-agnostic; planning quality differences surface in Moving and Stuck instead. GPT-OSS-20B records the lowest baseline active time ($\\approx\\!16\\%$), which SAI doubles to $\\approx\\!32\\%$, consistent with its collapsed success rates in S2, S3, and S5.\nL213: DeepSeek-R1 shows the sharpest SAI reduction in Moving ($34\\%\\!\\to\\!17\\%$), indicating more spatially precise assignments under structured authority. LLaMA-3.3-70B’s Stuck fraction falls from $24\\%$ to $14\\%$ under SAI, while GPT-4o is a notable counter-example whose Stuck fraction increases ($11\\%\\!\\to\\!16\\%$), showing that structured directives can introduce routing conflicts even when task completion improves.\nL214: ### IV-D Token efficiency\nL215: \nL216: cite74†Image: Refer to caption Figure 5: The token usage of Baseline. cite75†Image: Refer to caption Figure 6: The token usage of SAI. (The DeepSeek-R1 / S7 cell reflects a generation-budget timeout, not a token saving; see Section IV-D.)\nL217: Figure cite76†5 and Figure cite77†6 report average input tokens per agent call across scenarios. First, the two strongest models consume fewer tokens under SAI. GPT-4o and DeepSeek-R1 show consistent reductions across most scenarios. The most pronounced reductions occur in S6–S7: DeepSeek-R1 falls from 25,487 to 17,467 in S6 ($-$31%), and GPT-4o from 15,141 to 9,328 in S7 ($-$38%). DeepSeek-R1’s S7 value (473) is excluded as a generation-budget timeout, not a saving.\nL218: We attribute these reductions to the authority context providing sufficiently precise constraints that the model reaches valid assignments in fewer iterations, reducing the accumulated tool-call history that dominates prompt length in later epochs.\nL219: Second, the remaining models show mixed or increased consumption. Claude-Haiku, LLaMA-3.3-70B, and Qwen3-32B exhibit token increases under SAI in several scenarios (Qwen3-32B rises from 3,345 to 9,778 in S7) suggesting these models do not consistently exploit structured directives to prune their planning search, paying a token cost without a corresponding planning benefit.\nL220: Gemini-2.0-Flash records the lowest absolute counts in both conditions, but its task-completion failures indicate that low consumption here reflects insufficient planning depth rather than efficiency. S4, S6, and S7 are the high-cost scenarios in both heatmaps regardless of model or condition, confirming that scenario-driven complexity (i.e., late interrupts, capability matching, bottleneck congestion) sets a token floor that SAI does not eliminate.\nL221: Token efficiency and task-completion benefit are correlated: GPT-4o and DeepSeek-R1, which gain most from SAI in completion rate, also gain most in token economy.\nL222: ## V Findings and Discussion\nL223: \nL224: Finding 1: SAI benefit is stratified by observed capability. Some models gain across every dimension; others incur token overhead without commensurate planning gains, and model scale does not predict which is which.\nL225: The models fall into three empirical tiers. Tier 1 (GPT-4o, DeepSeek-R1) exploits the authority context to prune its planning search, achieving token reductions of up to 38% and sharper spatial assignment precision. Tier 2 (GPT-OSS-20B) recovers large completion gains in previously failing scenarios but remains sensitive to context complexity under late interrupts. Tier 3 (LLaMA-3.3-70B, Claude-Haiku, Qwen3-32B) shows mixed completion effects while consistently incurring token overhead.\nL226: Tier membership tracks neither parameter count nor model family: the 20B GPT-OSS and the 70B LLaMA-3.3 land in different tiers, and Qwen3-32B recovers from 0.00 to 1.00 in S8 yet rises from 3,345 to 9,778 tokens in S7 without a corresponding benefit. A further boundary case is DeepSeek-R1 in S7, where the authority context inflated the reasoning chain beyond its 4,096-token budget, producing complete planning failure—a risk not captured by standard tool-use benchmarks.\nL227: For operators, neither parameter count nor model family can substitute for direct measurement: generation budget must be verified per model against the expected authority-context length, making a lightweight qualification benchmark a necessary pre-deployment gate.\nL228: Finding 2: MCP as an authority layer, not just a tool bus. SAI’s asymmetric callability transforms MCP from a bidirectional tool bus into a one-directional authority channel: override tools are accessible only to the supervision interface, never to the LLM. Because schema validation is prior to LLM inference, malformed or adversarially injected directives are rejected before influencing planning—a guarantee that prompt-convention-based HitL designs [cite45†20 , cite46†22 ] cannot provide at the protocol level.\nL229: This architectural inversion has direct implications for agentic system security. Prompt injection attacks, where malicious content in tool outputs attempts to hijack agent behavior, are an active threat in deployed LLM systems. In LLM-driven network management or cloud orchestration, a successful injection that removes a Constraint override could silently disable a traffic engineering rule or bypass a resource quota.\nL230: SAI’s server-side validation firewall eliminates this vector: the authority context is writable only via a credentialed supervision interface, making tamper resistance an architectural property rather than a prompt-engineering convention. Furthermore, because the precedence rule ($C\\succ P\\succ S$) is evaluated deterministically at prompt-construction time, every authority decision is fully auditable without inspecting model internals.\nL231: This is a property essential for regulated deployment contexts such as network security operations and industrial automation.\nL232: Finding 3: Stuck-tick rate as a planning quality diagnostic. Working time remains near-uniform across all models (6–10%); planning quality differences manifest in the stuck fraction. LLaMA-3.3-70B’s stuck fraction falls from 24% to 14% under SAI, whereas GPT-4o’s rises from 11% to 16% despite unchanged or improved completion in most scenarios, closely mirroring the divergence in their assignment quality.\nL233: Task completion rate is a coarse metric that can mask persistent planning pathologies: a planner repeatedly assigning agents to infeasible targets will eventually complete all tasks while wasting substantial time in blocked and transit states. Stuck-tick rate, the fraction of ticks in which any agent is assigned but physically blocked, that directly quantifies this waste.\nL234: We propose it as a standard diagnostic complement to completion-based measures in LLM-driven multi-agent evaluation, noting its low instrumentation cost: it requires only a per-agent state flag per tick, available in any environment with collision or contention detection.\nL235: Limitations and Future Work. The current design assumes a single trusted supervisor; multi-operator deployments would require an arbitration layer to resolve conflicting overrides before the class-level precedence rule is applied. Domain-specific semantics may necessitate taxonomy extensions beyond the three current classes.\nL236: Beyond these structural limitations, SAI introduces a non-trivial prompt overhead that can destabilize models already operating at or near ceiling performance, as evidenced by Gemini-2.0-Flash’s regression in S2 and the drops observed for GPT-4o and LLaMA-3.3-70B in S7.\nL237: These degradations suggest that authority context injection can introduce competing directive signals that interfere with well-calibrated planners, and that the benefit–cost balance of structured injection is sensitive to baseline model priors and scenario constraint density; mitigating this may require adaptive context compression or selective directive rendering conditioned on model capacity.\n"
